window.OSAP_FT=window.OSAP_FT||{};window.OSAP_FT["1e"]={"https://theconversation.com/avoiding-the-f-word-australia-and-turkey-dance-around-fossil-fuels-at-cop31-summit-296418":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"04d974b1884e74538916552cf8cb725f2c5da48f637eab87f07606431e52f693","read":"https://theconversation.com/avoiding-the-f-word-australia-and-turkey-dance-around-fossil-fuels-at-cop31-summit-296418","bytes":188202,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Avoiding the ‘F word’: Australia and Turkey dance around fossil fuels at COP31 summit","by":[{"n":"Matt McDonald","r":"Professor of International Relations, The University of Queensland"}],"pub":"2026-11-13T04:21:18Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["One phrase has been glaringly absent from this year’s ",["COP31 climate summit","https://theconversation.com/plenty-of-reasons-for-hope-what-30-years-of-climate-talks-have-achieved-296398"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fossil fuels are not mentioned in the COP31 ",["priority areas","https://www.genevaenvironmentnetwork.org/resources/updates/climate-action-from-geneva-to-antalya-un-climate-change-conference-cop31/"],". Key speeches from the leaders of Australia and Turkey (Türkiye), co-hosts of the conference, have largely ",["failed to address","https://unfccc.int/news/transcript-of-press-conference-opening-remarks-today-of-cop31-president-president-of-negotiations-un"]," the emissions-shaped elephant in the room."]},{"k":"p","s":["That’s despite mounting pressure from ",["environmental groups","https://www.greenpeace.org/international/story/86619/cop31-antalya/"],", ",["Pacific nations","https://theconversation.com/australias-climate-minister-is-about-to-walk-a-diplomatic-tightrope-at-cop31-294565"]," and COP30 host nation ",["Brazil","https://ipam.org.br/cop31-ipam-highlights-lulas-call-for-action-against-the-climate-crisis/"]," to abandon coal, oil and gas and slash global emissions."]},{"k":"p","s":["So why does this matter? And what does it mean for climate action worldwide?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["‘Plenty of reasons for hope’: what 30 years of climate talks have achieved","https://theconversation.com/plenty-of-reasons-for-hope-what-30-years-of-climate-talks-have-achieved-296398"]]},{"k":"h","s":["A strange absence"]},{"k":"p","s":["In their opening speeches, neither Turkish President ",["Recep Tayyip Erdoğan","https://www.akparti.org.tr/en/main-slider-news/president-recep-tayyip-erdogan-delivered-a-speech-at-the-cop31-leaders-summit-opening-event-in-antalya-11-11-2026-15-00-00/"]," nor Australian Prime Minister ",["Anthony Albanese","https://www.pm.gov.au/media/cop31-leaders-summit-opening-ceremony"]," mentioned fossil fuels."]},{"k":"p","s":["At the official commencement of COP31, the Australian president of negotiations and environment minister, ",["Chris Bowen","https://unfccc.int/news/transcript-of-press-conference-opening-remarks-today-of-cop31-president-president-of-negotiations-un"],", twice uttered the words fossil fuels. Neither reference was tied to specific goals for a global fossil fuel phase out or weaning Australia, one of the world’s ",["largest exporters","https://theconversation.com/dug-up-in-australia-burned-around-the-world-exporting-fossil-fuels-undermines-climate-targets-236248"]," of coal and gas, off them. In his opening remarks, Turkey’s environment minister ",["Murat Kurum","https://unfccc.int/news/transcript-of-press-conference-opening-remarks-today-of-cop31-president-president-of-negotiations-un"]," didn’t mention them at all."]},{"k":"p","s":["This reluctance to spotlight fossil fuels is also reflected in the priority areas, or main aims, of COP31 that were agreed by the co-host nations."]},{"k":"p","s":["The headline goal is the “ ",["35-by-35","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-10/cop31-tingle-global-temperature-1-5c/107366402?utm_source=abc_news_app&utm_medium=content_shared&utm_campaign=abc_news_app&utm_content=link"],"” target to produce 35% of the world’s energy from electricity, increasingly using renewables, by 2035. Other priorities include halving the growth of ",["global waste","https://unfccc.int/news/un-secretary-general-s-remarks-to-the-leaders-roundtable-zero-waste-circularity-and-methane"]," and cutting energy consumption in the ",["building sector","https://unfccc.int/news/cop31-presidency-announces-new-targets-on-global-electrification-cutting-waste-resilient-cities"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet, nowhere in sight is a plan or timeline to eliminate fossil fuels."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Global carbon emissions may have peaked in 2026 – but only time will tell","https://theconversation.com/global-carbon-emissions-may-have-peaked-in-2026-but-only-time-will-tell-296152"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Why the reluctance?"]},{"k":"p","s":["Negotiators may point to previous COPs, where the very mention of fossil fuels has sparked contention. At COP30 in Brazil, major oil producers Russia and Saudi Arabia ",["fought hard","https://theconversation.com/cop30-petrostates-block-climate-deal-once-again-but-some-countries-are-taking-their-own-decisive-steps-to-phase-out-fossil-fuels-270580"]," to ensure fossil fuels did not feature in the final agreed text. In comparison, the 2035 electrification goal may be more likely to ",["achieve consensus","https://www.theage.com.au/environment/climate-change/harry-kewell-was-his-neighbour-paul-keating-was-his-hero-the-making-of-climate-change-minister-chris-bowen-20261030-p616rm.html?collection=p619gp&gb=1"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["But there’s a simpler explanation for this fossil fuel avoidance: any discussion of these fuels would undermine the credibility of the co-host nations as climate leaders, given their common reliance on coal, oil and gas."]},{"k":"p","s":["Australia is the world’s ",["third-largest exporter","https://www.climatecouncil.org.au/momentum-monitor-kpi/fossil-fuel-exports/"]," of fossil fuels, behind only Russia and the United States. These offshore emissions bring Australia’s total emissions ",["close to 5%","https://climateanalytics.org/publications/evaluating-the-significance-of-australias-global-fossil-fuel-carbon-footprint"]," of the global footprint. What’s more, Australia ",["continued approving","https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-11-09/global-emissions-footprint-overshadows-australias-image/107329288?utm_source=abc_news_app&utm_medium=content_shared&utm_campaign=abc_news_app&utm_content=link"]," new fossil fuel mining projects, even after its role at COP31 was confirmed."]},{"k":"p","s":["Turkey is not a fossil fuel exporter but remains a ",["major consumer","https://www.theenergymix.com/cop31-host-turkiye-co-president-australia-falling-short-on-clean-energy-electrification-pledge/"]," of them, ",["particularly coal","https://www.worldometers.info/coal/coal-consumption-by-country/"],". On the eve of COP31 talks, Turkish President Erdogan ",["was criticised","https://www.arabnews.com/middle-east/erdogan-hails-new-oil-find-ahead-of-cop31-speech-3010886"]," for celebrating the discovery of two significant new oil fields."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Dug up in Australia, burned around the world – exporting fossil fuels undermines climate targets","https://theconversation.com/dug-up-in-australia-burned-around-the-world-exporting-fossil-fuels-undermines-climate-targets-236248"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Not totally off the table"]},{"k":"p","s":["Abandoning fossil fuels clearly ",["isn’t a priority","https://www.japantimes.co.jp/environment/2026/11/04/climate-change/cop31-brazil-fossil-fuel/"]," of this year’s hosting team. But this doesn’t necessarily extend to other nations attending COP31."]},{"k":"p","s":["In the lead-up to COP31, civil society and environmental groups, including ",["Greenpeace","https://www.greenpeace.org/international/story/86619/cop31-antalya/"]," and ",["Amnesty","https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2026/11/cop31-world-leaders-must-break-fossil-fuels-grip-on-climate-policy/"],", voiced their disappointment that fossil fuels were largely left out of the official agenda. While recognising the headline goals as worthy aims, some organisations criticised the agenda as being not ambitious enough, given the scale of the climate crisis."]},{"k":"p","s":["Pacific countries have also pushed hard for an end to fossil fuels, notably through a ",["fossil fuel treaty","https://www.fossilfueltreaty.org/tassiriki-call"]," that would establish concrete plans and timelines. These small island nations have also declared Australia’s approval of new fossil fuel projects as “ ",["immoral and unacceptable","https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/01/australia-coalmine-decision-akin-to-drowning-its-pacific-neighbours-tuvalu-climate-minister-says?CMP=Share_iOSApp_Other"],"”. This criticism is particularly telling, given Australia made its co-hosting bid with the aim of spotlighting the climate vulnerability of its ",["Pacific neighbours","https://news.uq.edu.au/2026-11-what-you-need-know-about-cop31"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":[["Brazil","https://www.smh.com.au/politics/federal/fossil-fuels-must-go-the-way-of-the-dinosaurs-un-boss-pushes-australia-to-go-further-20261112-p61dfw.html"]," has also emerged as a vocal critic of this year’s co-hosts. After leading the preceding COP30 conference, Brazil formally presented its ",["global roadmap","https://cop30.br/en/news-about-cop30/roadmap-taff"]," for transitioning away from fossil fuels to several high-profile UN leaders, ",["making a splash","https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/11/1168775"]," on the second day of COP31 negotiations. The roadmap is a practical guide offering targets, timetables and other considerations to help countries shift away from fossil fuels. After speaking to the challenge of climate change, newly re-elected President ",["Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva","https://ipam.org.br/cop31-ipam-highlights-lulas-call-for-action-against-the-climate-crisis/"]," urged his COP31 audience to turn the roadmap into concrete action."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Australia’s climate minister is about to walk a diplomatic tightrope at COP31","https://theconversation.com/australias-climate-minister-is-about-to-walk-a-diplomatic-tightrope-at-cop31-294565"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Why does this matter?"]},{"k":"p","s":["This debate over whether to call out the world’s reliance on fossil fuels is problematic, not least because it points to a failure of global climate cooperation."]},{"k":"p","s":["The science is clear: greenhouse gas emissions from coal, oil and gas are driving ",["climate change","https://www.un.org/en/climatechange/science/causes-effects-climate-change"],". With the world on track to “ ",["overshoot","https://www.unep.org/en/publications/report/limiting-overshoot-navigating-exceedance-15degc-and-pathways-towards-return"],"” the Paris Agreement ",["target of 1.5°C","https://theconversation.com/the-world-will-soon-shoot-past-1-5-c-what-does-this-mean-294577"],", reducing and ultimately eliminating fossil fuel use must be a priority."]},{"k":"p","s":["But there is still hope. While fossil fuels may be a source of discomfort for Australia and Turkey, this debate could spur global climate action in other ways."]},{"k":"p","s":["Introducing the global roadmap, for instance, could make the comparatively modest 35-by-35 electrification goal more appealing, particularly to countries that remain reluctant to phase out fossil fuels. This “decarbonisation by stealth” approach could help lower global emissions. However, some nations may support the electrification goal but ",["still resist","https://www.climatechangenews.com/2026/11/10/cop31-bulletin-day-2-energy-day-off-to-slow-start-amid-transport-chaos"]," calls to get that electricity from renewables. In that sense, failing to directly target fossil fuels may lead to partial or imperfect climate action."]},{"k":"p","s":["Nevertheless, expect the “F word” – fossil fuels – to remain centre stage during the second week of COP31 and future climate summits. We simply can’t afford to keep kicking this dirty can down the road."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Yes, the COP climate talks are far from perfect. But they still hold the power for change","https://theconversation.com/yes-the-cop-climate-talks-are-far-from-perfect-but-they-still-hold-the-power-for-change-295350"]]}],"chars":6027},"https://theconversation.com/rnzs-budget-crisis-threatens-its-core-purpose-but-theres-an-obvious-solution-296293":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:16Z","sha":"5d5d721794b2719e201c7a20787afa190246ae023b4a01ca69087311d938f7a7","read":"https://theconversation.com/rnzs-budget-crisis-threatens-its-core-purpose-but-theres-an-obvious-solution-296293","bytes":179439,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"RNZ’s budget crisis threatens its core purpose. But there’s an obvious solution","by":[{"n":"Peter Thompson","r":"Associate Professor in Media and Communication, Te Herenga Waka — Victoria University of Wellington"}],"pub":"2026-11-12T20:33:56Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["“News and information you can trust,” says the voiceover before each RNZ news bulletin. Unfortunately, it seems the same couldn’t be said for the state broadcaster’s internal accounting."]},{"k":"p","s":["RNZ’s ",["2025-26 annual report","https://www.rnz.co.nz/assets/cms_uploads/000/000/556/RNZ_2025-2026_Annual_Report_Web.pdf"],", released on November 9, revealed a NZ$11.8 million deficit – well in excess of the projected $470,000 loss in its earlier ",["statement of performance expectations","https://www.rnz.co.nz/assets/cms_uploads/000/000/506/2025-2026_Statement_of_Performance_Expectations.pdf"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The accounting discrepancy (now subject to an ",["independent review","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2491845/treasury-wants-own-oversight-of-rnz-to-be-part-of-independent-review"],") is a huge embarrassment for the RNZ board and executive team. The consequences for RNZ’s ability to fulfil its public service remit are potentially dire."]},{"k":"p","s":["Management and board were apparently unaware of the problem until the ",["sudden resignation of the chief financial officer","https://www.rnz.co.nz/national/programmes/checkpoint/audio/2019055120/rnz-chief-financial-officer-resigns-and-leaves-job-on-same-day"]," in early October."]},{"k":"p","s":["A subsequent ",["external audit of the accounts","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2491845/treasury-wants-own-oversight-of-rnz-to-be-part-of-independent-review"]," found multiple accounting errors relating to land sales, the Auckland office relocation, operational expenses, and personnel and redundancy costs."]},{"k":"p","s":["A $5 million loan facility was also found to have been set up without ministerial approval, ",["in breach of the Crown Entities Act","https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/rnzs-unlawful-overdraft-was-signed-off-by-the-board/HLTXAIVNAJGAHBOX7RO3WP2YJU/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["These anomalies raise serious questions about the integrity of RNZ’s financial governance, including the role of the board and its audit and risk committee. But the backdrop to the budget blowout is, if anything, of even greater concern."]},{"k":"h","s":["Huge workforce cuts"]},{"k":"p","s":["As ",["RNZ’s statement","https://www.rnz.co.nz/media/337"]," acknowledged, the errors were not picked up in time to take corrective action during the financial year, meaning “RNZ did not reduce its cost base as it needed to in response to a 7% reduction in baseline funding”."]},{"k":"p","s":["That refers to the government’s 2025 budget, which cut RNZ’s annual funding by $4.6 million a year, to $62.3 million – a reduction of $18.4 million over four years. The 2026 budget further reduced RNZ’s budget by $1.4 million."]},{"k":"p","s":["In response, RNZ had already ",["planned to cut 20 to 30 positions","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/business/2485692/rnz-blowout-big-cuts-big-hit-to-reputation"]," by mid-2027 ( ",["18 of which","https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/2487361/media-minister-rules-out-coming-to-rnz-s-aid-after-budget-blowout-a-colossal-mistake"]," had already been agreed through a voluntary process)."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the unexpected $11.8 million shortfall is now projected to mean the loss of 58 positions by next March – roughly 16% of the workforce – along with at least a 5% cut to operating expenditure."]},{"k":"p","s":["RNZ maintains it has adequate reserves to sustain it through a period of transition. But it projects a further $7.8 million deficit in the 2026-27 financial year, after which it will return to a sustainable footing."]},{"k":"p","s":["As board chair Brent Impey has indicated, the crisis is not an existential threat to RNZ: the $11.8 million deficit mainly ",["comprises one-off losses","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVa24tVzkQw"],", including office relocation expenses and wrongly-accounted land sales."]},{"k":"p","s":["But if the board’s plan is to balance the books by the 2027-28 financial year, the concern is surely the impact this has on RNZ’s role as a public service media operation."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political hostility"]},{"k":"p","s":["In contrast to its financial management, RNZ’s actual performance has been very positive."]},{"k":"p","s":["In line with its ",["letter of expectations","https://www.rnz.co.nz/assets/cms_uploads/000/000/538/RNZ_response_to_Minister_regarding_LoE_2026_-_28_April_2026.pdf"]," from shareholding ministers, it has increased its online and ",["linear","https://www.nzherald.co.nz/entertainment/linear-tv-overtakes-streaming-are-kiwis-tired-of-endless-choices/OUVW6MJM4NG6LGQKYITKCWK4BU/"]," audiences, reaching 83% of New Zealanders aged 18-plus each month. The 2026 ",["JMAD Trust in News report","https://www.jmadresearch.com/_files/ugd/a95e86_b1937c0c5f82498088e43cbb5552215d.pdf"]," ranked RNZ the most trusted New Zealand news source."]},{"k":"p","s":["RNZ also played a civil defence role in 26 emergencies. It shared news content with dozens of other news media (within New Zealand and in the Pacific), and supported the expansion of the ",["Local Democracy","https://www.rnz.co.nz/ldr"]," reporting scheme to 22 regional reporters."]},{"k":"p","s":["According to a survey quoted in its annual report, 79% of New Zealanders agreed it is important for the country to have a public media organisation."]},{"k":"p","s":["In this light, it’s also important the apparent failure of financial governance not be conflated with wider criticism of RNZ as an essential civic institution."]},{"k":"p","s":["But the latest budget shock has undoubtedly played into the hands of government politicians whose open hostility towards RNZ is well known."]},{"k":"p","s":["New Zealand First leader Winston Peters (at the time also deputy prime minister) directly ",["threatened the organisation’s funding","https://newsroom.co.nz/2025/06/25/the-many-hands-that-held-the-knife-against-rnz/"],". Meanwhile, ACT’s David Seymour (a shareholding minister of RNZ) criticised presenter appointments, alleged political bias and suggested cuts to RNZ’s budget. He also ",["called for an overhaul of the board","https://newsroom.co.nz/2026/05/08/hold-my-beer-seymour-takes-a-wild-swing-at-public-media/"]," and management."]},{"k":"p","s":["Tempting as it may be for RNZ’s ideological critics to point to recent financial failings as vindication of their concerns, it does not logically follow that RNZ’s performance as a public media operation is in question."]},{"k":"p","s":["On the contrary, what does merit review is the previous coalition government’s decision to repeatedly cut RNZ’s budget at the same time as demanding constant improvements in its programming performance."]},{"k":"h","s":["An obvious solution"]},{"k":"p","s":["Whatever the complexion of the new government, a bailout would likely be a politically untenable response to a crisis ostensibly of RNZ’s own making."]},{"k":"p","s":["But that is not necessary anyway. As Impey has noted, the sale of a transmitter site in Auckland is due to ",["bring in $51 million","https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jVa24tVzkQw"]," in the next financial year. The only question is how much of this revenue RNZ will be permitted to retain by the Treasury."]},{"k":"p","s":["Even a quarter of that sum would be sufficient to offset the $11.8 million deficit. If that were permitted, there would be no need for additional redundancies and operational cost-cutting. RNZ could continue to deliver the services for which it is justifiably funded."]},{"k":"p","s":["There is therefore an obvious solution and a straightforward political decision to be made. But it will require the support of RNZ’s incoming shareholding ministers."]},{"k":"p","s":["Regardless of which parties comprise the next government, they have a responsibility to ensure RNZ’s civic, democratic and cultural functions are not undermined."]}],"chars":5544},"https://theconversation.com/canada-can-develop-infrastructure-and-protect-biodiversity-293521":{"r":"conversation","at":"2026-11-17T16:30Z","sha":"2f65e097dadbd2f8af9c388b0ee817775d19e3f3164e0d1a43237c34f3e594f2","read":"https://theconversation.com/canada-can-develop-infrastructure-and-protect-biodiversity-293521","bytes":189710,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Canada can develop infrastructure and protect biodiversity","by":[{"n":"Andrew Nicholas Kadykalo","r":"Adjunct Professor, School of Environment, Resources and Sustainability (SERS), University of Waterloo; Trent University"},{"n":"Elizabeth Ann Nyboer","r":"Assistant Professor, Department of Fish and Wildlife Conservation, Virginia Tech"},{"n":"Steven J Cooke","r":"Canada Research Professor, Conservation Physiology, Carleton University"}],"pub":"2026-11-01T13:08:02Z","mod":"","blocks":[{"k":"p","s":["Nearly 200 countries recently came together in Armenia for the ",["2026 United Nations Biodiversity Conference","https://cop17yerevan.am/en"]," to review progress toward halting and reversing biodiversity loss. Many of these governments will soon convene again in Turkey to discuss ",["efforts to tackle climate change at COP31","https://unfccc.int/cop31"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["In 2022, parties to the United Nations Convention on Biological Diversity adopted the ",["Kunming-Montréal Global Biodiversity Framework","https://www.cbd.int/gbf"]," aimed at reversing biodiversity loss by 2030 and put nature on a path to recovery by 2050. An early review of the first 36 national plans found that ",["none had created a plan that fully met two targets","https://theconversation.com/with-just-5-years-to-go-the-world-is-failing-on-a-vital-deal-to-halt-biodiversity-loss-249841"],": to restore 30 per cent of degraded ecosystems, and to conserve 30 per cent of lands and waters, by 2030."]},{"k":"p","s":["By the end of 2025, Canada had conserved ",["only 14 per cent of its land and fresh water and 15 per cent of its oceans","https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/services/environmental-indicators/conserved-areas.html"],". In 2025, ",["the federal environment commissioner found","https://open.canada.ca/data/en/info/73a47fc9-d43b-480f-9eca-d83380f8e699"]," that the government ",["was not on track to meet its interim target of 25 per cent by 2025","https://www.canada.ca/en/auditor-general/media-room/canada-is-not-meeting-its-commitments-to-protect-and-conserve-land-and-inland-water.html"],", a target it went on to miss. A ",["companion audit","https://www.canada.ca/en/auditor-general/our-work/audit-reports/establishing-marine-protected-areas.html"]," reached the same conclusion for oceans."]},{"k":"p","s":["As a ",["party to the biodiversity convention","https://www.canada.ca/en/environment-climate-change/news/2022/12/canada-helps-lead-the-world-to-agreement-on-the-monumental-kunming-montreal-global-biodiversity-framework.html"],", Canada has a responsibility to conserve its ",["wilderness","https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-018-07183-6"],", ",["irrecoverable carbon","https://doi.org/10.1038/s41893-021-00803-6"],", ",["intact forests","https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.1600821"]," and ",["lakes","https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms13603"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["However, the Canadian government is also pursuing infrastructure and resource development, with plans to fast-track approvals for major projects and build ",["“at speeds not seen in generations.”","https://www.canada.ca/en/privy-council/major-projects-office.html"]," It has also made ",["significant cuts to public science","https://evidencefordemocracy.ca/canada-is-cutting-public-science-the-risks-will-not-be-invisible-forever/"]," and faced criticism ",["over cuts to public engagement and consultation, as well as access to information","https://www.hilltimes.com/2026/08/03/the-canadian-government-is-cutting-out-meaningful-public-consultations-and-info-access/513412/"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["We recently ",["published a study","https://doi.org/10.1002/2688-8319.70334"]," on how environmental research informs policy and practice through knowledge co-production. We interviewed 84 senior environmental professionals working at the science–policy interface across Canada, including federal, provincial and territorial governments and environmental non-profit organizations. We asked how environment-related evidence informs decisions and what enables or hinders knowledge co-production with a wide range of partners."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read more: ",["Budget cuts at Environment and Climate Change Canada threaten Arctic science","https://theconversation.com/budget-cuts-at-environment-and-climate-change-canada-threaten-arctic-science-276606"]]},{"k":"h","s":["What makes evidence count in policy"]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada faces a critical moment to show that rapid infrastructure growth does not have to come at the expense of nature and biodiversity goals. Doing so will require the deliberate use of the best available evidence to inform decisions."]},{"k":"p","s":["It will also require inclusive, collaborative governance rooted in trust, early relationship-building and sustained co-operation among governments, academia, non-governmental organizations and Indigenous and local communities."]},{"k":"p","s":["The biodiversity framework commits countries to ",["integrate biodiversity into decision-making at all levels","https://www.cbd.int/gbf/targets/14"],", ensure that access to the ",["best available data and knowledge guide action","https://www.cbd.int/gbf/targets/21"]," and guarantee inclusive, equitable ",["participation and representation in governance","https://www.cbd.int/gbf/targets/22"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The interviews we conducted predate the current push to fast-track major projects, but the barriers people described are the very ones that efforts to accelerate project approvals and development could make worse. This research, recently published, is especially relevant given Canada’s efforts to accelerate infrastructure development while meeting its environmental commitments."]},{"k":"p","s":["Interviewees told us that making information count in effective environmental policy depends on more than bringing people together. They pointed to building trust, jointly defining research questions, involving decision-makers and Knowledge Holders (including practitioners and Indigenous Peoples) early, and being transparent about how evidence is produced and interpreted."]},{"k":"p","s":["As one interviewee explained, when people are involved from the beginning, “even if the results are unexpected, they can say: ‘What you did made sense.’” Another interviewee similarly noted that knowledge co-production “shortens the path to use” by involving those who need or can benefit from the knowledge in its production from the outset."]},{"k":"p","s":["Trust, respect and recognition were the most frequently mentioned themes associated with successful knowledge co-production and evidence-based conservation in our study, followed by funding and resources."]},{"k":"p","s":["A ",["new global review of research on evidence use in conservation","https://besjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/1365-2664.70565"]," found a similar pattern, where relationships developed among scientists and decision-makers, the relevance of evidence, and the capacity and resources of organizations were the most frequently reported to influence evidence use."]},{"k":"p","s":["These findings highlight the importance of ",["early relationship-building, shared goals, clear communication and sustained cross-sector collaboration","https://doi.org/10.1002/2688-8319.70334"]," — all of which require time and ",["institutional support","https://doi.org/10.3389/fcosc.2021.693129"],". The barriers that the people we interviewed raised most often were the opposite, including tight budgets and limited staff time."]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet these are precisely the conditions that can be undermined when governments fast-track projects while reducing scientific capacity, public engagement and consultation."]},{"k":"p","s":["In a ",["companion paper based on the same suite of interviews","https://doi.org/10.1111/csp2.426"],", we outlined how evidence syntheses — research that critically reviews, collates and summarizes available knowledge — can increase confidence in the evidence and resulting decisions."]},{"k":"p","s":["We also found that understandings of ",["what counts as evidence are shifting","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsci.2025.104176"],". Decision-makers are increasingly looking to draw on diverse forms of evidence, but considerable uncertainty remains about how best to bring them together in a respectful and meaningful way."]},{"k":"h","s":["What knowledge co-production brings"]},{"k":"p","s":["Yet there is growing recognition that diverse evidence ",["does not always translate into its use","https://doi.org/10.1002/pan3.10194"],". Most environmental professionals we interviewed still viewed peer-reviewed natural science ",["as the gold standard","https://doi.org/10.1016/j.envsci.2025.104176"],". Indigenous, local and community knowledge, and social science, were frequently discussed but tended to be neglected."]},{"k":"p","s":["Indigenous knowledge was the form of evidence interviewees most often said they were not using. Many wanted to draw on it but lacked the know-how, capacity or time to do so. Others noted that mandates to include Indigenous knowledge often came without practical guidance."]},{"k":"p","s":["This matters when decisions are fast-tracked. Interviewees told us that short timelines already keep harder-to-access forms of evidence out of decisions."]},{"k":"p","s":["Under even tighter timelines, environmental evidence may be overlooked entirely, or decision-makers may rely on what is most readily available: in-house science and familiar experts. This can favour perspectives already represented while sidelining other relevant evidence and knowledge held by those most affected, including Indigenous rights-holders and local communities."]},{"k":"p","s":["Knowledge co-production offers one way to address this imbalance. Knowledge co-production means developing knowledge alongside those who will use it. Rather than working in isolation and sharing findings at the end, researchers collaborate with governments, Indigenous organizations, communities, industry and other partners from the outset to shape research questions, interpret evidence and apply findings to real-world challenges."]},{"k":"p","s":["By involving partners from the outset, decision-makers and knowledge holders can jointly identify the questions that matter, determine which forms of evidence are needed, and discuss how different knowledge systems can inform decisions. Rather than retrofitting Indigenous and/or local knowledge to support predetermined decisions, knowledge co-production creates space to consider them meaningfully from the beginning."]},{"k":"p","s":["Canada can pursue ambitious social and economic goals while meeting its environmental commitments. Success will ",["require evidence-informed decisions that respect Indigenous rights, are legally sound and work in practice","https://theconversation.com/elbows-up-in-canada-means-sustainable-resource-development-255669"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["Our research shows that trust, early relationship-building and sustained collaboration help deliver durable decisions without unnecessary barriers to development. Efforts to accelerate decision-making should not come at the expense of these foundations."]}],"chars":7956},"https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea":{"r":"govuk","at":"2026-11-17T16:38Z","sha":"9caf778143692aa0afad3fad9a660efda14fc5eea35023d20e2c816a0310c8ca","read":"https://www.gov.uk/api/content/foreign-travel-advice/guinea","bytes":58677,"x":"fulltext v1","title":"Guinea travel advice","by":[{"n":"Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office","r":""}],"pub":"2013-03-10T00:00:00+00:00","mod":"2026-08-12T16:34:39+01:00","blocks":[{"k":"h","s":["Warnings and insurance"]},{"k":"h","s":["Before you travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["No travel can be guaranteed safe. Read all the advice in this guide. You may also find it helpful to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for women travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/advice-for-women-travelling-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read our ",["guide on disability and travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/disabled-travellers"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["general advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"]]},{"k":"li","s":["read about ",["safety for solo and independent travel","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/solo-and-independent-travel"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see ",["advice on volunteering and adventure travel abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/safer-adventure-travel-and-volunteering-overseas"]]},{"k":"hs","s":["Travel insurance"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you choose to travel, research your destinations and ",["get appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"],". Insurance should cover your itinerary, planned activities and expenses in an emergency."]},{"k":"h","s":["About FCDO travel advice"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) provides advice about risks of travel to help you make informed decisions. Find out ",["more about FCDO travel advice","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["Travel Aware on Instagram","https://www.instagram.com/accounts/login/?next=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.instagram.com%2Ftravelaware%2F&is_from_rle"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Entry requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["This information is for people travelling on a full ",["‘British citizen’ passport","https://www.gov.uk/types-of-british-nationality"]," from the UK. It is based on the UK government’s understanding of Guinea’s current rules for the most common types of travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities in Guinea set and enforce entry rules. If you’re not sure how these requirements apply to you, contact the ",["Guinean Embassy in the UK","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/foreign-embassies-in-the-uk"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Passport validity requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guinea, your passport must have an ‘expiry date’ at least 6 months after the date you arrive."]},{"k":"p","s":["Check with your travel provider that your passport and other travel documents meet requirements. ",["Renew your passport","https://www.gov.uk/renew-adult-passport/renew"]," if you need to."]},{"k":"p","s":["You will be denied entry if you do not have a valid travel document or try to use a passport that has been reported lost or stolen."]},{"k":"h","s":["Dual nationals"]},{"k":"p","s":["When returning to the UK, British citizens must carry a valid ",["British passport","https://www.gov.uk/browse/abroad/passports"]," or ",["certificate of entitlement","https://www.gov.uk/right-of-abode/apply-for-a-certificate-of-entitlement"],". See ",["Dual citizenship","https://www.gov.uk/dual-citizenship"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Visa requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["You must have a visa to visit Guinea for up to 90 days. Make sure you apply for the correct visa – British nationals have been refused entry with the wrong one."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Applying for a visa"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can ",["apply online for an e-visa for private and business travel","https://www.paf.gov.gn/visa"],". You must apply several days in advance and upload scanned documents, including a yellow fever vaccination certificate. When you arrive in Guinea, take your e-visa registration receipt to the entry checkpoint, where border officials will issue your visa."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine requirements"]},{"k":"p","s":["To enter Guinea, you must have a certificate to prove you’ve had a yellow fever vaccination if you’re coming from a ",["country listed as a transmission risk","https://nathnacyfzone.org.uk/factsheet/65/countries-with-risk-of-yellow-fever-transmission"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["For more details about medical entry requirements and recommended vaccinations, including yellow fever, see ",["TravelHealthPro’s Guinea guide","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/97/guinea#Vaccine_Recommendations"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Customs rules"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are strict rules about goods you can take into or out of Guinea. You must declare anything that may be prohibited or subject to tax or duty."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Taking money into Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is no limit on cash you can bring into Guinea. When you leave, you cannot take more than 100,000 Guinean francs, or 5,000 US dollars or the equivalent in euros."]},{"k":"h","s":["Safety and security"]},{"k":"h","s":["Terrorism"]},{"k":"p","s":["There is a high threat of terrorist attack globally affecting UK interests and British nationals, including from groups and individuals who view the UK and British nationals as targets. Stay aware of your surroundings at all times."]},{"k":"p","s":["UK Counter Terrorism Policing has information and advice on ",["staying safe","https://www.counterterrorism.police.uk/runhidetell/"]," and what to do in the event of a terrorist attack. Find out ",["how to reduce your risk from terrorism while abroad","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/reduce-your-risk-from-terrorism-while-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Terrorism in Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["Terrorist attacks in Guinea cannot be ruled out."]},{"k":"p","s":["Attacks could be indiscriminate including in places visited by foreigners. Stay aware of your surroundings, keep up to date with local media reports and follow the advice of local authorities."]},{"k":"h","s":["Political situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["While the political environment is currently stable, there is ongoing tension between the government and opposition parties."]},{"k":"p","s":["Protests linked to economic challenges, such as electricity shortages, occur from time to time. Tensions can rise quickly. Public gatherings and demonstrations can start with little or no warning and may turn violent. In the past, protestors have damaged public buildings, and there have been fatalities and injuries among protestors and bystanders."]},{"k":"p","s":["Stay away from demonstrations, especially near military installations, and monitor reliable, local and international media for updates. If you see crowds forming or signs of unrest, move promptly to a safe location."]},{"k":"h","s":["Security situation"]},{"k":"p","s":["Border areas of Guinea are often tense with an increased military presence. Security has improved but things can still be tense, particularly at big events such as elections."]},{"k":"h","s":["Crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["For assistance or to report a crime in Conakry, call the local police Commissaire on +224 622 86 94 71."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Vehicle crime"]},{"k":"p","s":["Criminals posing as police or military, armed with military weapons, have robbed drivers and their passengers at gunpoint, particularly at night. Violent carjackings are a threat, especially in the outlying suburbs of Conakry. Reduce the risk of vehicle crime by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["getting security advice before travelling by road"]},{"k":"li","s":["travelling in convoy"]},{"k":"li","s":["using reputable travel operators and taxis, arranged in advance"]},{"k":"li","s":["keeping doors locked and windows closed while driving"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are occasional reports of hold-ups and armed robbery on the roads to:"]},{"k":"li","s":["Mamou"]},{"k":"li","s":["Faranah"]},{"k":"li","s":["Kissidougou"]},{"k":"li","s":["Guékédou"]},{"k":"li","s":["Macenta"]},{"k":"li","s":["N’zérékoré"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you plan to arrive in Conakry on a flight after dark, arrange your airport transfer before you travel."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Protecting yourself and your possessions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Break-ins are common, and burglars may be armed. There is also a high level of street crime – mugging, pickpocketing and bag-snatching. Take precautions by:"]},{"k":"li","s":["ensuring your accommodation is secure and has security guards"]},{"k":"li","s":["taking a pre-arranged taxi if you are changing money or visiting a bank"]},{"k":"li","s":["not wearing expensive-looking jewellery or watches and keeping mobiles out of sight"]},{"k":"li","s":["avoiding emptier parts of towns and cities"]},{"k":"li","s":["not walking alone at night"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Scams and extortion"]},{"k":"p","s":["The British Embassy in Conakry is aware of police extorting cash from foreigners and Guineans."]},{"k":"p","s":["Take particular care if you trade gold and diamonds. This trade attracts criminal gangs and can involve kidnapping and extortion. There are reports of trading scams involving diamonds, gold export and gold certification."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Detention"]},{"k":"p","s":["The criminal justice process in Guinea falls below international standards. If you are arrested or detained due process may not be followed and delays should be expected. Pre-trial detention is common and can last for many months."]},{"k":"p","s":["Local prison conditions are harsh, often without regular food and water. The main prison in Conakry suffers from serious overcrowding."]},{"k":"h","s":["Laws and cultural differences"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Personal ID"]},{"k":"p","s":["Always carry photo ID such as a passport."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Illegal drugs"]},{"k":"p","s":["Do not become involved with illegal drugs of any kind."]},{"k":"p","s":["If you commit a drugs crime, you are likely to be prosecuted. There are heavy penalties for those convicted."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Using cameras in secure areas"]},{"k":"p","s":["Photography and filming in many areas is highly restricted. This includes:"]},{"k":"li","s":["military bases and checkpoints"]},{"k":"li","s":["the President’s residence and offices"]},{"k":"li","s":["police and gendarmerie HQs"]},{"k":"p","s":["It’s illegal to photograph or film anything of strategic value such as airports and bridges. Do not photograph military or police installations or government buildings, or you could be arrested."]},{"k":"p","s":["Individuals may prefer not to be photographed. If in doubt, ask, or do not take pictures."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Money"]},{"k":"p","s":["Credit cards are not widely used in Guinea and obtaining hard currency is very difficult. There are a few ATMs in Conakry that accept foreign cards, but they give only small amounts of currency. They are also unreliable. Banks in Conakry accept international credit cards and exchange money."]},{"k":"p","s":["Exchanging foreign currency on the street is illegal. Foreign nationals have been arrested and held in military custody for using unofficial money changers even for small amounts, including at the airport."]},{"k":"hs","s":["LGBT+ travellers"]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex sexual activity is illegal. Penalties range from 6 months to 3 years in prison and a fine of 500,000 to 1 million Guinean francs."]},{"k":"p","s":["Same-sex relationships are not widely accepted or understood. Read more ",["advice for LGBT+ travellers","https://www.gov.uk/lesbian-gay-bisexual-and-transgender-foreign-travel-advice"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Precious stones"]},{"k":"p","s":["You need an export licence to take precious stones out of Guinea. Gem smuggling is illegal, and anyone convicted can expect a severe prison sentence. Local prison conditions are harsh."]},{"k":"h","s":["Transport risks"]},{"k":"hs","s":["Road travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you are planning to drive in Guinea, see ",["information on driving abroad","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["You’ll need to have both the ",["1949 version of the international driving permit (IDP)","https://www.gov.uk/driving-abroad/international-driving-permit"]," and your UK driving licence with you in the car. You cannot buy an IDP outside the UK, so get one before you travel."]},{"k":"p","s":["Most major hotels and travel agencies offer cars for hire, with a driver if required."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Driving conditions"]},{"k":"p","s":["Road travel can be hazardous, especially during the rainy season from May to October. Roads, even within Conakry and other principal towns, can be flooded and become impassable."]},{"k":"p","s":["The standard of road maintenance is low. Many roads are unmetalled and have deep potholes as damage caused during the rainy season goes unrepaired."]},{"k":"p","s":["Fuel is not always available at petrol stations. Carry an emergency supply on long journeys."]},{"k":"p","s":["British Embassy staff avoid road travel outside the Conakry region at night. If staff choose to hire transport for personal journeys, they are encouraged to satisfy themselves in advance of the vehicle’s roadworthiness and the driver’s competence. The use of moto-taxis and shared taxis/tuk-tuks is not advised."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Buses and taxis"]},{"k":"p","s":["Taxis and long-distance buses are generally poorly maintained, overcrowded, and the drivers are often unqualified."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Roadblocks and checkpoints"]},{"k":"p","s":["Police and local militia maintain checkpoints across the country. They check vehicle and passenger documentation and baggage. Corruption and extortion are common at roadblocks. Occasionally, checkpoints can be a pretext for armed robbery."]},{"k":"p","s":["The authorities may close the Guinea-Sierra Leone border at short notice."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Sea travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["There have been reports of piracy attacks against vessels in Guinean territorial waters."]},{"k":"h","s":["Health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Before you travel check that:"]},{"k":"li","s":["your destination can provide the healthcare you may need"]},{"k":"li","s":["you have ",["appropriate travel insurance","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-insurance"]," for local treatment or unexpected medical evacuation"]},{"k":"p","s":["This is particularly important if you have a health condition or are pregnant."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency medical numbers"]},{"k":"p","s":["In remote areas you will have to arrange local transport. In Conakry, call local clinics."]},{"k":"p","s":["Clinique Pasteur: +224 621 35 01 01 or +224 664 26 98 53"]},{"k":"p","s":["International Clinic: +224 654 05 55 55 or +224 654 09 99 99"]},{"k":"p","s":["Clinique Ambroise Paré: +224 631 40 10 40"]},{"k":"p","s":["Hôpital de l’Amitié Sino-Guinéenne: +224 621 08 88 62"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your insurance company quickly if you’re referred to a medical facility for treatment."]},{"k":"h","s":["Vaccine recommendations and health risks"]},{"k":"p","s":["At least 8 weeks before your trip:"]},{"k":"li","s":["check the latest ",["vaccine recommendations for Guinea","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/97/guinea#Vaccine_Recommendations"]]},{"k":"li","s":["see where to get vaccines and whether you have to pay on the ",["NHS travel vaccinations page","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/travel-vaccinations/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["See ",["what health risks you’ll face in Guinea","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/country/97/guinea"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["yellow fever"]},{"k":"li","s":["Zika virus"]},{"k":"li","s":["malaria and dengue"]},{"k":"li","s":["chikungunya"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are occasional outbreaks of Ebola in Guinea. The last one was in 2021. Cholera and malaria are also present in Guinea and have similar early symptoms to Ebola. You can read ",["more information on Ebola and similar diseases","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/68/viral-haemorrhagic-fever"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Medication"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are some well-stocked pharmacies in Conakry but few outside the capital. Check that medicine is genuine."]},{"k":"p","s":["The legal status and regulation of some medicines prescribed or bought in the UK can be different in other countries."]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["best practice when travelling with medicines on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/43/medicines-abroad"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Healthcare in Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["Medical facilities are poor. Equipment is basic and often not sterile. Carry basic medical supplies and a first aid kit."]},{"k":"p","s":["For serious medical treatment, medical evacuation to Europe is necessary. Make sure you have adequate travel health insurance and accessible funds to cover the cost of any medical treatment abroad and repatriation."]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has a ",["list of medical providers in Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guinea-list-of-medical-facilities"]," where some staff will speak English."]},{"k":"h","s":["Travel and mental health"]},{"k":"p","s":["Read ",["FCDO guidance on travel and mental health","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/foreign-travel-advice-for-people-with-mental-health-issues"],". There is also ",["mental health guidance on TravelHealthPro","https://travelhealthpro.org.uk/factsheet/85/travelling-with-mental-health-conditions"],"."]},{"k":"h","s":["Getting help"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office (FCDO) cannot provide tailored advice for individual trips. Read this travel advice and carry out your own research before deciding whether to travel."]},{"k":"h","s":["Emergency services in Guinea"]},{"k":"p","s":["There are no emergency services in Guinea. If you need urgent medical assistance, contact a clinic in Conakry:"]},{"k":"p","s":["Clinique Pasteur: +224 621 35 01 01 or +224 664 26 98 53"]},{"k":"p","s":["Clinique Ambroise Paré: +224 631 40 10 40"]},{"k":"h","s":["Contact your travel provider and insurer"]},{"k":"p","s":["Contact your travel provider and your insurer if you are involved in a serious incident or emergency abroad. They will tell you if they can help and what you need to do."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Refunds and changes to travel"]},{"k":"p","s":["For refunds or changes to travel, contact your travel provider. You may also be able to make a claim through insurance. However, insurers usually require you to talk to your travel provider first."]},{"k":"p","s":["Find out more about ",["changing or cancelling travel plans","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/about-foreign-commonwealth-development-office-travel-advice#changing-or-cancelling-travel-plans"],", including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["where to get advice if you are in a dispute with a provider"]},{"k":"li","s":["how to access previous versions of travel advice to support a claim"]},{"k":"h","s":["Support from FCDO"]},{"k":"p","s":["FCDO has guidance on staying safe and what to do if you need help or support abroad, including:"]},{"k":"li","s":["finding English-speaking ",["lawyers","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/lawyers?country=Guinea"],", ",["funeral directors","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/funeral-directors?country=Guinea"]," and ",["translators and interpreters","https://find-a-professional-service-abroad.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/find/translators-interpreters?country=Guinea"]," in Guinea"]},{"k":"li","s":["dealing with a ",["death in Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guinea-bereavement-death-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["being ",["arrested or imprisoned in Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guinea-prisoner-pack"]]},{"k":"li","s":["getting help if you’re a ",["victim of crime","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/victim-of-crime-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["what to do if you’re ",["in hospital","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/in-hospital-abroad"]]},{"k":"li","s":["if you’re ",["affected by a crisis","https://www.gov.uk/guidance/how-to-deal-with-a-crisis-overseas"],", such as a terrorist attack"]},{"k":"h","s":["Get travel advice updates"]},{"k":"p","s":["Sign up to ",["get email notifications","https://www.gov.uk/foreign-travel-advice/guinea/email-signup"]," when this travel advice is updated."]},{"k":"p","s":["Follow FCDO:"]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on X","https://x.com/fcdotravelgovuk"]]},{"k":"li","s":[["travel advice on Facebook","https://www.facebook.com/FCDOTravel/"]]},{"k":"p","s":["You can also ",["contact FCDO online","https://www.contact.service.csd.fcdo.gov.uk/posts/guinea/british-embassy-conakry"],"."]},{"k":"hs","s":["Help abroad in an emergency"]},{"k":"p","s":["If you’re in Guinea and you need emergency help from the UK government, contact the ",["British Embassy in Conakry","https://www.gov.uk/world/organisations/british-embassy-conakry"],"."]},{"k":"p","s":["The British Embassy in Conakry can only provide limited emergency consular assistance. Access to the Embassy is strictly by appointment only. Please send an email to BritishEmbassy.Conakry@fcdo.gov.uk for consular assistance or to make an appointment. If you need urgent consular help, call +44 (0) 1908 516 666."]},{"k":"hs","s":["FCDO in London"]},{"k":"p","s":["You can call FCDO in London if you need urgent help because something has happened to a friend or relative abroad."]},{"k":"p","s":["Telephone: 020 7008 5000 (24 hours)"]},{"k":"p","s":[["Find out about call charges","https://www.gov.uk/call-charges"]]},{"k":"h","s":["Risk information for British companies"]},{"k":"p","s":["The Overseas Business Risk service offers ",["information and advice for British companies operating in Guinea","https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/overseas-business-risk-guinea"]," on how to manage political, economic, and business security-related risks."]}],"chars":14594,"change":"Updated information about health risks you’ll face in Guinea (‘Health’ page)."}};
